  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...More strong storms possible especially Tuesday.\n\n\nWith a moist airmass remaining over the region for the next\ncouple of days, there will be chances for showers and\nthunderstorms Monday, Tuesday for most of the area, and \nWednesday across the Piedmont. With minimal deep layer shear\nacross the area today, the main threat for thunderstorms will be\nstrong to severe winds. SBCAPE values only look to increase to\naround 1500-2000 J/Kg through peak heating this afternoon as a\nmid level cloud deck looks to develop across the region. This\nlooks to inhibit some destabilization through the afternoon and\nevening. Nevertheless, dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s\nacross the area will aid in widespread shower and thunderstorm\nactivity, especially across mountain locations, and along the\nBlue Ridge, which should push east into the Piedmont through \nthe evening. \n\nBy Tuesday, a strong summer time front looks to push through the\narea that is associated with a potent shortwave trough ejecting\nsoutheast out of Canada and through the Great Lakes region by\nTuesday afternoon. This will fire widespread thunderstorm\nactivity across the region, with all modes of severe weather\nlooking possible. While wind and hail currently look to remain\nthe greatest threats, there is a small tornado threat north of\n460 as low level shear will be elevated ahead of the front. With\nthe shortwave pushing into the region, deep layer shear looks to\nincrease into the 30-40 knot range, and SBCAPE values look to\nincrease into the 2200-2700 J/Kg range by the afternoon and\nevening hours. The largest uncertainty at this time, is the\noverall timing of the front on Tuesday. CAM guidance currently\nhas it arriving beyond peak diurnal heating, which may limit\nsome severity of storms across the region. If the front arrives\nearlier in the evening, expect to see more hazardous weather\nacross the region, but if these storms arrive later towards the\nearly overnight hours, the severity of these storms will be much\nmore reduced. \n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned front looks to get hung up in a\neast/west orientation along the VA/NC border providing a focal\npoint for shower/thunderstorm activity primarily south of 460.\nSpecifics on severity and impacts expected will come with future\nupdates. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures should turn more seasonable by the\nmiddle of the week after a frontal passage.\n\nStrong summertime front passing through the region Tuesday night \nwill bring a large pattern change to the area starting \nWednesday. Behind the passage of the front, upper level\ntroughing looks to settle across the eastern conus through much\nof the work week. This will keep 500mb heights much lower across\nthe region leading to overall cooler temperatures. Surface high\npressure coming out of Canada will also lower dewpoints into the\n50s and 60s which will make it feel much more comfortable\noutside during the peak heat of the day. Highs look to only\nclimb into the upper 70s to low 80s across the mountains, and\nmid to upper 80s across the Piedmont starting Wednesday, and\ncontinuing through Saturday before temperatures look to increase\nagain on Sunday of next week. Shower and thunderstorm chances\nbeyond the frontal passage Tuesday night will primarily be\nrelegated to mountain locations, and look to remain isolated, \nwhere orographical lift can enhance development during peak \nheating.",
